  "problem": "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3} & =\\frac{7}{4(x+3)}+\\frac{5}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+5(4)}{4(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4(x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n4(x+3)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$",
